The null checks are unnecessary because `delete` on a null pointer is already a no-op. Resetting the members to `nullptr` is also unnecessary: the object's lifetime ends with this destructor, so nothing can legally read them afterwards. Removing both saves a couple of branches and stores. This is a micro-change; little measurable speedup is expected.

```cpp
DeveloperFieldDefinition::~DeveloperFieldDefinition()
{
    delete mesg;
    delete developer;
}
```